'''HMS ''Maori''''' was one of five ships of the third batch of {{sclass2|Tribal|destroyer (1905)|0}} [[destroyer]]s built for the [[Royal Navy]] in the first decade of the twentieth century. Completed in 1909, she spent her career in British waters. During the [[World War I|First World War]], she served in the [[North Sea]] and the [[English Channel]] with the [[6th Destroyer Flotilla]]. She struck a [[naval mine|mine]] in the [[North Sea]] on 7 May 1915 off [[Zeebrugge]], [[Kingdom of Belgium|Belgium]], and sank.

==Description==
Ordered as part of the 1907–1908 Naval Programme, the third batch of ''Tribal''-class destroyers were improved versions of the earlier-batch ships.<ref>Chesneau & Kolesnik, p. 72</ref> ''Maori'' [[Displacement (ship)|displaced]] {{convert|1026|LT|t|lk=on}} at normal load and {{convert|1150|LT|t}} at [[deep load]]. She had an [[length overall|overall length]] of {{convert|285|ft|m|1}}, a [[beam (nautical)|beam]] of {{convert|27|ft|1|in|m|1}} and a [[draft (hull)|draught]] of {{convert|8|ft|11|in|m|1}}. The ship was powered by a single [[steam turbine]] set which drove three [[propeller shaft]]s using steam provided by six [[Yarrow boiler]]s. The turbine was rated at {{convert|15500|shp|lk=on}} and was intended to give a maximum speed of {{convert|33|kn|lk=in}}.<ref name=f1>Friedman, p. 294</ref> During her [[sea trial]]s ''Maori'' reached {{convert|33.2|kn}} from {{cvt|26199|shp}}.<ref>March, p. 85</ref> The third-batch Tribals carried a maximum of {{convert|162|LT|t}} of [[fuel oil]] that gave them a range of {{convert|1640|nmi|lk=in}} at {{convert|15|kn}}. Their crew numbered 71 officers and [[naval rating|ratings]].<ref name=f1/>

The ships were armed with a pair of [[BL 4-inch Mk VIII naval gun|BL {{convert|4|in|mm|0|adj=on}} Mk VIII gun]] in single mounts, one on the [[forecastle]] and the other on the [[stern]]. Their [[torpedo]] armament consisted of two rotating [[torpedo tube]]s for [[British 18 inch torpedo|18-inch (450 mm)]] torpedoes, one mount between the two forward [[funnel (ship)|funnel]]s and the other on the stern.<ref>March, p. 84</ref>

==Construction and career==
''Maori'' was [[laid down]] by [[William Denny and Brothers]] at its [[Greenock]] [[shipyard]] on 6 August 1908, [[Ceremonial ship launching|launched]] on 24 May 1909 and completed in November.<ref name=f2>Friedman, p. 305</ref> On commissioning, ''Maori'' joined the [[1st Destroyer Flotilla]], based at [[Harwich]], replacing the [[River-class destroyer (1903)|River-class destroyer]] {{HMS|Dee|1903|2}}.<ref>{{cite magazine|title=Naval Matters—Past and Prospective |magazine=The Marine Engineer and Naval Architect |date=December 1909 |pages=173–174}}</ref> By March 1913, ''Maori'' was part of the [[4th Destroyer Flotilla]] of the First Fleet.<ref>{{cite magazine |title=Fleets and Squadrons in Commission at Home and Abroad: Flotillas of the First Fleet |magazine=The Navy List |date=March 1913 |page=269a |url=https://digital.nls.uk/british-military-lists/archive/94246518 |via=National Library of Scotland |accessdate=6 March 2020}}</ref>

